That kind of depends on whether you care about playing the *same*
games that the Windows users are all playing.  A typical Linux
distribution comes with several thousand games.  Granted, most of
them are pretty simple (and some of them are incredibly simple), but
there are also some really quite good ones.  Nonetheless, yeah, if
you go out to the store and buy one of the commercial twitch games
that the Windows gamers are all excited about, you will frequently
have a hard time getting it to run on Linux and sometimes may not be
able to do so even with Wine, even if you know what you're doing.

Oh, one other thing:  Ubuntu probably has a ready-to-use package in
its repositories for the Qt build of NetHack.  Debian certainly does.
So if your goal is just to *have* the Qt build and be able to play
it, you won't need to compile it.  You only need to compile it if
you want to roll your own (e.g., because you want certain patches
that are not included in vanilla, or you want to make your own
patches, etc.)


